<p class="lt-chem-36401">The mechanism of aminolysis follows a typical nucleophilic acyl substitution. The amine nucleophile attacks the carbonyl carbon of the acid chloride forming an alkoxide tetrahedral intermediate. The -Cl leaving group is eliminated, allowing the carbonyl bond to be reformed. Because amines are neutral nuleophiles a protonated amide is produced after this step. In the last step of the mechanism, a second amine acts as a base, removing a proton, and allowing for the amide product to be formed.</p>

