</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/21:_Carboxylic_Acid_Derivatives-_Nucleophilic_Acyl_Substitution_Reactions/21.04:_Chemistry_of_Acid_Halides" class="mt-section" id="section_27"><span id="Conversion_of_Acid_Chlorides_to_Ketones:_Gilman_Reagents"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-36401 editable">Conversion of Acid Chlorides to Ketones: Gilman Reagents</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-36401">When acid chlorides are reacted with Grignard reagents the ketone intermediate is difficult to isolate because the addition of a second equivalent of the highly reactive Grignard reagent rapidly occurs. Organocuprates however are significantly less reactive than organolithium and organomagnesium reagents and when an acid chloride is reacted with a diorganocuprate (Gillman) reagent (R<sub>2</sub>CuLi), a ketone product is produced in excellent yields.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/21:_Carboxylic_Acid_Derivatives-_Nucleophilic_Acyl_Substitution_Reactions/21.04:_Chemistry_of_Acid_Halides" class="mt-section" id="section_28"><span id="General_Reaction_6"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-36401 editable">General Reaction</h4>

